<title>DENMARK: Danish Oct North Sea oil production rises 3.6 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Oct North Sea oil production rises 3.6 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1996-11-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields rose 3.6 percent in October from September, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>It told Reuters that oil production was 960,000 tonnes in October or 227,620 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 219,754 bpd in September.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.</p>
<p>Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p> Field			Oct 96	 Sept 96	 Oct 95</p>
<p> Dan			   66.6	    67.3	    65.4</p>
<p> Kraka			  5.2	     5.3	     8.1</p>
<p> Regnar			 0.8	     0.9	     1.3</p>
<p> Gorm			  53.5	    52.9	    41.5</p>
<p> Skjold			32.1	    30.9	    36.6</p>
<p> Rolf			   3.9	     4.4	     4.5</p>
<p> Dagmar			 0.4	     0.4	     0.5</p>
<p> Tyra			  23.7	    20.3	    28.4</p>
<p> Valdemar		     2.2	     2.6	     3.3</p>
<p> Roar			   8.2	     7.8		 -</p>
<p> Svend			 30.4	    26.9		 -</p>
<p> TOTAL			227.6	   219.8	   189.6</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc own 46 and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-- Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45 33 96950</p>
